DIRECTIVE — PROJECT TERAX-C ADDITION: SUBAGENT DELEGATION SYSTEM
Add a subagent delegation system to the Terax-C build. This is the same working model as Kilo Code's agent system: the main orchestrator never burns its context doing grunt work. It dispatches subagents that go execute a task in a fresh, isolated context, do the work, and come back with results and information only. The orchestrator reads the returned result, not the process. This gets built once, the right way, as a core production feature of the product — not a bolted-on fast version.

WHAT YOU BUILD
Orchestrator: The main agent process. Holds the user's session, the plan, and the state. It does not read files wholesale, does not run exploratory commands, does not wade through build output. Its context stays reserved for decisions and coordination. It decomposes work into discrete tasks and delegates.

Subagent spawner: A dispatcher that launches isolated subagent processes — each one a fresh context, a defined role, a bounded task, and a hard result contract. Subagents run concurrently where tasks are independent. Each subagent gets: the task description, the exact scope it may touch, the result format it must return, and nothing else. No subagent inherits the full session context. No subagent talks to another subagent — all coordination flows through the orchestrator.

Result contract: Every subagent returns a compact, structured result: status (done/failed/blocked), the evidence gathered (verbatim output, file diffs, measurement numbers), and a summary that fits the orchestrator's context budget. If a subagent's result is too large, it writes the full evidence to a file and returns the path plus a digest. The orchestrator never receives raw oceans of output.

Task classes, each its own subagent role: exploration and survey (read a module, return its interface map), implementation (write a specific module to spec), verification (run a build or test, return pass/fail with verbatim output), and measurement (capture timings, memory, file sizes). The orchestrator assigns roles; a role defines what the subagent is allowed to do. Implementation subagents never modify files outside their named scope — enforcement is real, checked at the file-operation layer, not promised.

Persistence and recovery: Subagent dispatches and results are journaled. If the orchestrator dies, a restarted orchestrator reads the journal and knows what was dispatched, what returned, and what never came back. In-flight work is re-dispatched or resumed — never silently lost, never duplicated.

Integration: This system is built into the product itself and used by the product's AI agent panel — so the end user's agent sessions get the same architecture: main agent keeps context, subagents do the legwork and return results. It is also the working method for finishing the Terax-C build itself: the orchestrator you run uses these subagents to complete the remaining build work. The system is proven by using it, live, on the real build.

PROOF LAW
Live internal runtime data is the only proof. No echo, no printf, no narration, no mocked results. Prove the system by dispatching real subagents doing real work: a subagent reads a real backend module and returns its real interface map; a subagent implements a real module and returns real diffs; a subagent runs the real build and returns the verbatim pass/fail output; a subagent measures real memory and latency and returns real numbers. Show the orchestrator's context consumption with and without delegation — live measurements proving the system does what it exists to do: the orchestrator's context stays small while the total work completed is large. Journal recovery proven by killing the orchestrator mid-task and showing the restarted one pick up correctly, verbatim.

QUALITY BAR
Production, industrial, once-and-done. Concurrent subagents without races or deadlocks. Hard scope enforcement at the operation layer. Bounded result sizes guaranteed by the contract. Clean teardown of all subagent processes on exit — no orphans, ever. Works lean on a 2-core, 3.3GB machine. Unknown is UNKNOWN — never invent an interface, result, or capability; if it cannot be measured, it is reported UNKNOWN.

